   a + b &= m \\
   ab &= 2
   \end{align*}

Also,
   \begin{align*}
   a + \frac{1}{b} + b + \frac{1}{a} &= p \\
   \left(a + \frac{1}{b}\right) \left(b + \frac{1}{a}\right) &= q
   \end{align*}

First, find $p$ in terms of $m$:
\[ a + \frac{1}{b} + b + \frac{1}{a} = a + b + \frac{1}{a} + \frac{1}{b} = a + b + \frac{a + b}{ab} = m + \frac{m}{2} = \frac{3}{2} m \]

Now we find $q$:
   \begin{align*}
   \left(a + \frac{1}{b}\right) \left(b + \frac{1}{a}\right) &= ab + 1 + 1 + \frac{1}{ab} \\
   &= ab + \frac{1}{ab} + 2 \\
   &= 2 + \frac{1}{2} + 2 \\
   &= \boxed{\frac{9}{2}}
   \end{align*}

